// Break loop: fix it while it's running.
//
// Acceptance target, written before the features exist. The scenarios run
// `raymarch.loko`, a one-row shader whose `shade` method produces a negative
// brightness for rays that miss the sphere; `check-sample`, two calls deeper,
// signals `<bad-sample>`. Nothing in the program handles `<bad-sample>`.
//
// What each step proves, and what it depends on:
//
// 1. `:run main` does not unwind. The signal searches for a handler while
//    every frame is still live, finds none (the `handle` in `pixel` is for
//    `<skip-pixel>` only), and the REPL acts as the default handler for
//    unhandled conditions. Depends on cross-call conditions with handler
//    search before unwinding (branch `conditions`, in progress): `signal`
//    with no lexically enclosing `handle`, dynamic handler search, and the
//    built-in `use-value`, `retry` and `abort` restarts.
// 2. The break loop shows the condition, the live stack innermost first
//    with source locations and the code unit each frame runs (`@N`), and
//    the restarts on offer. `skip-pixel` is program-defined: an enclosing
//    `with restart` clause whose class does not match the condition is
//    offered as a restart rather than taken as a handler.
// 3. The suspended frames, locals and the condition are EOP objects
//    (`frame:N`, `condition:N`, `restart:N`), inspected with the existing
//    `:inspect` and `:affordances` commands. Depends on EOP.
// 4. `:edit method:m0` (the direct spelling of the `edit-body` affordance)
//    replaces the method in place. A broken edit is rejected and leaves both
//    the definition and the suspended computation untouched; the corrected
//    edit is checked by soft typing, which rechecks the dispatch site in
//    `pixel`, before it is installed. `frame:1` keeps running the
//    superseded code unit `method:m0@1`. Depends on execution "code units"
//    (planned): a frame pins the unit it entered, and redefinition installs
//    a new unit instead of patching code under a live frame.
// 5. `retry frame:1` unwinds to the call that created `frame:1` and makes
//    it again; dispatch now picks `method:m0@2`, and `main` returns 1.5.
// 6. After `:reload` brings the bug back, `use-value` resumes the signal in
//    `frame:0` with a supplied value (typed: a string is refused), and the
//    run completes with 1.75. A third run is aborted through the structured
//    affordance rather than a REPL command: everything unwinds, the
//    `block ... cleanup` in `render` runs, and the REPL is back at top level.
// 7. Choosing a restart is one structured affordance, `choose-restart` on
//    the condition, with arguments typed by the chosen restart's signature.
//    The REPL break loop is only the human chooser. The second scenario
//    drives the same program through `interloko_lab` with a scripted
//    chooser (`choices.toml`) and no REPL at all. A future oracle chooser,
//    a model picking the restart with its choice recorded as host evidence
//    for replay, plugs into the same seam and records the same choice shape;
//    nothing here should need rework for it.
//
// Invented surface (none of it exists yet): `:frames`, `:restart <n|name>
// [args...]`, `:edit <object-id>`, `:invoke condition:N choose-restart
// <restart> [name: value...]`, the `frame:`, `condition:` and `restart:`
// object ids (scoped to the active break level), `@N` code-unit suffixes,
// and `interloko_lab run --chooser <choices.toml>`.
//
// Format: `=== repl` and `=== lab` start a scenario. In a repl scenario,
// `> ` lines are stdin. In a lab scenario, each `$ interloko_lab ...` line
// is one invocation. `! ` lines are expected stderr, other lines expected
// stdout. Commands run from the `interloko_repl` package root. Blank lines
// and `//` lines are ignored.

=== repl: human chooser in the REPL break loop

// Steps 1 and 2: load the program and run it into the signal.
> :load tests/fixtures/break_loop/raymarch.loko
loaded tests/fixtures/break_loop/raymarch.loko (10 forms)
